Electrical discharge machining is frequently adopted for difficult-to-machine nickel‑chromium alloys such as Hastelloy C‑22 because conventional machining is often ineffective for these materials. {{cite:S1#c1}} The physical and mechanical properties of the tool electrode directly influence process efficiency, machining accuracy, and surface roughness in EDM of such alloys. {{cite:S1#c1}} Recent studies report a marked trend toward using graphite electrodes, motivated by low raw-material cost, good machinability, and high sublimation temperature. {{cite:S1#c1}}  

Two recent experimental investigations specifically evaluated POCO graphite electrodes with fine (≈1 µm) and coarser (≈10 µm) grain sizes when machining Hastelloy C‑22. {{cite:S1#c1}} Those studies found that finer-grained graphite increases process efficiency and reduces tool wear, while coarser-grained graphite yields higher material removal rates but also higher relative tool wear; fine grains produced lower surface roughness (Ra). {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S1#c1}} The mechanism proposed links detached graphite grains to discharge stability: grain detachment affects spark stability and thus process efficiency. {{cite:S3#c1}}  

Across the cited experiments, electrical parameters emerge repeatedly as the dominant drivers of MRR, TWR and Ra. {{cite:S1#c1}} Both discharge current (peak current) and pulse duration (pulse‑on time) are reported as the main factors controlling MRR and Ra for the graphite electrodes studied. {{cite:S1#c1}} One factorial/Taguchi study on a corrosion‑resistant superalloy (with cryogenic treatments) likewise identified peak current and pulse‑on time among the most influential parameters, with ANOVA attributing the largest shares of variance in MRR and Ra to peak current. {{cite:S2#c1}} A Taguchi/ANOVA study on titanium also found amperage to be the most effective factor for MRR and TWR. {{cite:S8#c1}} For electrode geometry and wear phenomena during machining of Hastelloy B2, current and pulse duration strongly affect electrode taper, radius, and wear length, with unstable sparks producing rounding of sharp edges. {{cite:S4#c1}}  

Several works survey and test auxiliary methods to mitigate high tool wear, low MRR, and poor surface integrity in EDM. {{cite:S6#c1}} Approaches include cryogenic treatment, powder‑mixed dielectrics, ultrasonic assistance, and process variants; modelling and optimisation studies (ANN, MOGA, Taguchi) have been used to predict and reduce electrode wear and overcut in other material systems. {{cite:S6#c1}} {{cite:S7#c1}} Electrical discharge coating (EDC) is discussed as a related surface‑modification technology, summarising process parameters and coating characteristics, though not specifically applied to Hastelloy electrodes in the provided sources. {{cite:S5#c1}}  

