The evidence on graphite electrodes shows grain size influences both process efficiency and surface finish in EDM of Hastelloy C-22. Experimental comparisons using POCO graphite with 1 µm and 10 µm grain sizes report that larger-grained graphite yields higher material removal rates but also higher relative tool wear, while finer-grained graphite produces lower surface roughness. {{cite:S1#c1}} Graphite with miniaturized grain size is reported to increase process efficiency and reduce tool wear, and detached graphite grains are said to affect discharge stability and process effectiveness; these observations were used to develop predictive mathematical models for MRR, relative TWR and Ra under negative polarity EDM of Hastelloy C‑22. {{cite:S3#c1}}

Across studies of nickel‑based and other difficult‑to‑machine alloys, discharge current (peak current) and pulse duration (pulse‑on time) consistently emerge as the principal process parameters driving MRR and surface roughness. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}} One factorial/Taguchi investigation found peak current to be the dominant factor controlling both MRR and Ra—accounting for the largest percentage of explained variance in that experiment—while optimal combinations included lower pulse‑on times for improved Ra and MRR in cryogenically treated samples. {{cite:S2#c1}} ANOVA and regression analyses in EDM studies of other alloys likewise identify amperage as the most influential factor for MRR and TWR. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Evidence on electrode wear behaviour and dimensional effects highlights that current and pulse duration strongly affect electrode geometry and wear morphology. {{cite:S4#c1}} Thermal diffusion from unstable sparks can transform sharp electrode edges into hemispherical ends, increasing electrode wear length and altering taper and radius. {{cite:S4#c1}} Reviews of EDM adaptations enumerate multiple strategies—cryogenic treatment, powder‑mixed dielectric, ultrasonic assistance and others—aimed at reducing TWR, increasing MRR, or improving surface integrity, indicating an active area of experimental mitigation approaches. {{cite:S6#c1}} {{cite:S7#c1}}

The reviewed material discusses electrical discharge coating technologies and surface modification by EDM processes but does not provide data on copper electrodes or on composite electrodes used specifically against Hastelloy in terms of TWR, MRR, or parameter sensitivity. [[NEEDS SOURCE: copper electrode performance on Hastelloy C‑22]] [[NEEDS SOURCE: composite electrode comparisons with graphite/copper on Hastelloy]]

Overall, studies supply detailed findings for graphite electrodes on Hastelloy C‑22 and identify the key controlling parameters (current, pulse duration) and mitigation techniques, but they leave unresolved the comparative performance of composite versus conventional (graphite or copper) electrodes on Hastelloy C‑22, a direct gap in the literature that existing graphite‑focused and general EDM reviews approach but do not fill. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S6#c1}}

