Electrical discharge machining of Hastelloy alloys shows strong sensitivity to the electrode material and the machining parameters, with multiple studies linking electrode physical properties and process settings to material removal rate (MRR), tool wear rate (TWR), and surface roughness (Ra). Experimental work on Hastelloy C-22 using POCO graphite electrodes with different grain sizes found that larger-grained graphite increases process efficiency (higher MRR) but also increases relative tool wear, while finer-grained graphite produced lower surface roughness. {{cite:S1#c1}} The same trend is reiterated in a later study that introduced fine-grained (1 µm) and coarser (10 µm) POCO graphite electrodes and concluded that fine-grained graphite increases process efficiency and reduces tool wear, with detached graphite grains affecting discharge stability and process effectiveness. {{cite:S3#c1}}  

Discharge current and pulse duration (pulse-on time) repeatedly emerge as the dominant electrical parameters controlling MRR, TWR and Ra in the cited experiments. Nowicki et al. identified discharge current and pulse duration as the main factors determining MRR and Ra for both fine- and coarse-grained graphite, and found the time interval (pulse-off) to be dominant for relative graphite wear. {{cite:S1#c1}} Other parametric studies on superalloys and difficult-to-machine materials likewise report peak current (amperage) and pulse-on time as the most influential factors: one Taguchi–ANOVA study found peak current to explain the largest shares of variance for MRR and Ra, and another EDM investigation reported amperage as the most effective factor across MRR, TWR and surface metrics. {{cite:S2#c1}} {{cite:S8#c1}} Ramabalan et al. observed that current and pulse duration greatly affect electrode wear and edge transformation, noting that unstable sparks and heat diffusion reshape sharp electrode features during EDM of Hastelloy B2. {{cite:S4#c1}}  

Several mitigation and enhancement strategies reported in the literature alter electrode behaviour or the machining environment to improve TWR and dimensional accuracy. Reviews and experimental studies discuss combining EDM with cryogenic treatments, powder-mixed dielectrics, ultrasonic assistance, and coating techniques to modify surface integrity and performance metrics; these works frame such combinations as responses to the persistent challenges of high TWR, low MRR and poor surface quality. {{cite:S6#c1}} {{cite:S5#c1}} Studies on electrode treatment and dielectric modification (for other alloys) report substantial reductions in electrode wear and dimensional inaccuracy when using treated electrodes or nanopowder-mixed dielectrics, indicating potential routes to improve EDM outcomes. {{cite:S7#c1}}  

The reviewed sources focus heavily on graphite electrodes and on electrical/temporal process parameters, but direct comparative data for copper electrodes in EDM of Hastelloy are not present in the collected passages. [[NEEDS SOURCE: copper electrode performance data for Hastelloy]]  

Similarly, explicit experimental comparisons between composite (engineered) electrodes and conventional electrodes for Hastelloy machining are absent from the provided material. [[NEEDS SOURCE: composite versus conventional electrode comparisons for Hastelloy]]

