The evidence on graphite electrodes shows that grain size strongly influences machining efficiency, tool wear and surface finish in EDM of Hastelloy C‑22. Experimental work using POCO graphites with 1 µm (AF‑5) and 10 µm (S‑180/EDM‑180) grain sizes reports higher process efficiency but greater relative tool wear for coarser graphite, while finer‑grained graphite yields lower surface roughness. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}} The same studies developed empirical or mathematical models linking machining parameters to MRR, TWR and Ra for these specific graphite grades. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}}

Multiple sources identify discharge current (peak current or amperage) and pulse duration (pulse‑on time) as primary drivers of MRR and surface roughness in EDM. Experimental designs applying factorial, Taguchi and ANOVA analyses conclude that peak current most strongly affects MRR and Ra, and that pulse duration is likewise influential; one Taguchi analysis quantified peak current as accounting for the majority of variance in MRR and Ra for treated and untreated specimens in a cryogenic‑treatment study. {{cite:S2#c1}} {{cite:S8#c1}} Studies on graphite Hastelloy machining also report that current and pulse duration dominate MRR and Ra for both fine and coarse grain electrodes, while the time interval (pulse‑off) chiefly governs relative tool wear for graphite. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}}

Work addressing electrode shape change and wear mechanisms shows that high currents and long pulse durations promote extensive electrode degradation and geometric transformation. Square electrodes used to produce square holes in Hastelloy B2 rapidly blunt and evolve into hemispherical ends due to unstable sparking and heat diffusion, with current and pulse duration greatly affecting electrode taper, radius and wear length. {{cite:S4#c1}} Broader reviews of EDM emphasise persistent challenges of high TWR, low MRR and poor surface integrity caused by continuous sparking, and they summarise a range of mitigation strategies including cryogenic treatment, powder‑mixed EDM and ultrasonic assistance. {{cite:S6#c1}} {{cite:S7#c1}}

Several studies explore process modifications and predictive modelling to improve electrode performance and dimensional accuracy. Fine‑grained graphite studies propose mathematical models for parameter selection to balance efficiency, wear and roughness, while nanopowder and cryogenic electrode treatments have been modelled with ANNs and optimisation algorithms to reduce electrode wear and overcut in other materials. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S7#c1}} However, these investigations focus on specific electrode types (graphite grades) or on electrode treatments in different material contexts rather than directly comparing conventional (e.g., copper or brass) and composite electrodes on Hastelloy. [[NEEDS SOURCE: copper and composite electrode data]]

The literature therefore leaves unresolved whether composite electrodes outperform conventional electrodes (copper, brass, graphite) for EDM of Hastelloy in terms of TWR, MRR and resulting surface integrity; the closest direct evidence pertains to graphite grain effects and parameter sensitivities on Hastelloy C‑22 and to general electrode‑wear dynamics under varying currents and pulse durations. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S4#c1}}

