Selective Laser Melting (SLM) produces near-full-density metallic parts by layer-wise melting and fusion of powder with a high-power laser, enabling near net-shape components with up to 99.9% relative density. {{cite:S1#c1}} Metal AM, and SLM in particular, imparts unique microstructural features in steels—hierarchical subgrains and fine precipitates—that can enhance strength, wear and corrosion resistance compared with conventional routes, while also acting as an intrinsic heat treatment that induces in situ phase transformations and tempering phenomena. {{cite:S6#c1}}

As-built SLM maraging steel commonly shows a very fine martensitic microstructure produced by extremely high cooling rates. Tan et al. observed submicron grain sizes (0.31 μm on average) indicating cooling rates up to 10^7 K/s and reported a martensitic matrix containing dense needle-shaped Ni3X (X = Ti, Al, Mo) nanoprecipitates that account for age hardening. {{cite:S3#c1}} Ahmadkhaniha et al. similarly found a fine-grained martensitic structure in as-printed maraging steel, with isotropic microstructure across build sections and a relatively high surface roughness (Ra ≈ 10 μm). {{cite:S8#c1}} These as-built features combine high strength with residual stresses and surface roughness that influence subsequent properties. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S8#c1}}

Post-build heat treatments substantially modify the microstructure and properties. Systematic heat-treatment studies detected phase-transformation temperatures and applied aging to develop precipitation strengthening and to relieve residual stress, with heat treatments producing the maximum nano-hardness associated with precipitation and also affecting corrosion potentials. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S8#c1}} Godec et al. investigated combinations of solution treatment, aging and plasma nitriding, showing that prior heat treatment influences the morphology and chemistry of thermochemical layers and that nitriding performed without prior aging led to cracking in the compound layer. {{cite:S2#c1}} These reports indicate that heat-treatment history dictates precipitate state, residual stress relief and compatibility with follow-up surface treatments. {{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S2#c1}}

Austenite reversion (reversed austenite) after intercritical or tempering treatments has been identified in SLM maraging steels and linked to both mechanical and corrosion behaviour. Khan et al. tempered specimens near austenite-forming temperatures and found reversed austenite nucleating mainly along martensite lath boundaries, promoted by local Ni enrichment from intermetallic dissolution. {{cite:S4#c1}} They report that this reversed austenite correlates with improved pitting resistance compared to as-printed, aged, or homogenized specimens. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Corrosion studies in chloride environments indicate that microstructure and surface condition strongly control performance. Khan et al. performed electrochemical tests in 3.5 wt% NaCl and found that the 720 °C intercritical-tempered specimen exhibited higher pitting potential and lower corrosion current density and rate than other conditions, concluding that reversed austenite enhances pitting resistance. {{cite:S4#c1}} Ahmadkhaniha et al. compared corrosion behaviour of as-printed and heat-treated SLM maraging steel with wrought counterparts and found that despite finer microstructure the corrosion behaviour in heat-treated conditions was similar, with precipitation playing a primary role in both hardness and corrosion response; they also showed electropolishing markedly reduced surface roughness. {{cite:S8#c1}} Godec et al. linked prior heat treatment to improved corrosion resistance after plasma nitriding, while nitriding without prior aging produced defects that impair performance. {{cite:S2#c1}}

[[NEEDS SOURCE: quantitative comparison of pitting rates across heat treatments]]

Selective Laser Melting (SLM) produces near fully dense maraging-steel parts by layerwise laser melting and rapid solidification, a process known to impart unique microstructural features such as very fine prior-β and martensitic substructures due to extremely high cooling rates. {{cite:S1#c1}} These process-induced features act as an intrinsic heat treatment in some AM steels, promoting hierarchical (sub)grains and fine precipitates that modify mechanical and corrosion-related properties relative to conventionally produced counterparts. {{cite:S6#c1}}

Studies of as-built SLM 18Ni-300 (grade 300) maraging steel consistently report a fine martensitic matrix with very small grain/subgrain sizes and in-situ precipitation. Tan et al. detected submicron grain sizes (~0.31 μm average) and dense, needle-shaped Ni3X precipitates in the as-fabricated martensitic matrix, attributing these features to cooling rates on the order of 10^7 K/s and linking the precipitates to subsequent age hardening. {{cite:S3#c1}} Ahmadkhaniha et al. likewise report a fine-grained martensitic structure in the as-printed condition and note the absence of strong texture or anisotropy across build directions. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Heat treatments alter this baseline microstructure through precipitation, residual-stress relief, and possible phase reversion. Tan et al. show that deliberate heat treatments induce strengthened precipitate distributions, reduce residual stresses, and somewhat reduce mechanical anisotropy compared with the as-fabricated state. {{cite:S3#c1}} Godec et al. explored combinations of solution treatment, aging and plasma nitriding, finding that prior heat treatment affects the morphology and chemistry of subsequent nitride layers and the associated wear and corrosion response. {{cite:S2#c1}} Ahmadkhaniha et al. compared SLM and wrought maraging steel after heat treatments and reported similar nano-hardness and corrosion behaviour in heat-treated conditions, with aging producing maximum nano-hardness and the lowest corrosion potentials; they identify precipitation as the dominant factor governing both hardness and corrosion response. {{cite:S8#c1}}

Austenite reversion during intercritical tempering has been investigated for its mechanical and corrosion effects. Khan et al. tempered SLM maraging steel near austenite-forming temperatures and observed reversed austenite precipitated mainly along martensite lath boundaries; they attribute nucleation to local Ni enrichment from intermetallic dissolution. {{cite:S4#c1}} Importantly, that study directly links reversed austenite to improved pitting resistance: the specimen tempered at 720 °C showed higher pitting potential, lower corrosion current density, and lower corrosion rate in 3.5 wt% NaCl than as-printed, aged, and homogenized samples without reversed austenite. {{cite:S4#c1}}

Reviews of AM steels emphasise both potential corrosion benefits and remaining gaps. Haghdadi et al. summarise that AM-induced fine grains and precipitates can enhance corrosion resistance relative to conventional steels but note challenges remain in achieving acceptable ductility and fatigue behavior. {{cite:S6#c1}} Afkhami et al. highlight that many process and post-process variables (orientation, heat treatment, surface quality) influence performance and identify gaps in understanding that also affect corrosion-relevant properties. {{cite:S5#c1}}

[[NEEDS SOURCE: Detailed comparison of chloride-corrosion mechanisms by microfeature]]

