Selective Laser Melting (SLM) builds parts by melting and fusing metallic powders layer by layer using a high-power laser, producing near-full-density components suitable for functional testing and property studies. {{cite:S1#c1}}

Evidence shows as-built SLM maraging steel exhibits a very fine martensitic microstructure with nanoscale precipitates formed in situ by the rapid thermal cycles of the process. {{cite:S3#c1}} Electron microscopy in one study revealed submicron grains (0.31 μm average) and abundant needle-shaped Ni3X (X = Ti, Al, Mo) nanoprecipitates within a martensitic matrix, which account for age hardening in the as-fabricated state. {{cite:S3#c1}} A complementary microstructural survey found a fine-grained martensitic structure with no preferred texture and similar morphology across build sections in as-printed SLM maraging steel. {{cite:S8#c1}} Layerwise thermal histories in SLM act as intrinsic heat treatments, producing hierarchical subgrains and fine precipitates that distinguish AM microstructures from conventionally processed steels. {{cite:S6#c1}}

Studies that compare heat-treated conditions report that deliberate post-build aging, solution treatment and intercritical tempering modify precipitate populations, residual stress, and phase fractions. {{cite:S3#c1}} One investigation tempered specimens near the austenite-forming range (intercritical treatment at 720°C) and found reversed austenite precipitated mainly along martensite lath boundaries, nucleated where local Ni enrichment followed dissolution of intermetallics. {{cite:S4#c1}} Another work explored the interaction of solution treatment, aging and subsequent plasma nitriding on SLM 18Ni-300, reporting that prior heat treatment influenced the morphology and integrity of nitrided layers and that nitriding without prior aging led to cracks in the compound layer. {{cite:S2#c1}}

Corrosion performance in chloride media appears sensitive to these microstructural changes and surface condition. {{cite:S6#c1}} One experimental study measured pitting behaviour in 3.5 wt% NaCl and reported that the 720°C-tempered specimen with reversed austenite exhibited a higher pitting potential, lower corrosion current density, and lower corrosion rate than as-printed, aged, or homogenized specimens without reversed austenite. {{cite:S4#c1}} Comparative testing of SLM and wrought maraging steel after heat treatment found similar nano-hardness and corrosion behaviour, with precipitation state controlling both hardness and corrosion potential; electropolishing substantially reduced SLM surface roughness and was assessed for its effect on corrosion. {{cite:S8#c1}} The combined study of nitriding and heat treatment concluded that thermochemical treatment following appropriate prior heat treatments can improve wear and corrosion resistance, whereas inappropriate sequences caused defects in the nitride layer. {{cite:S2#c1}}

Despite these findings, the literature leaves unresolved how specific SLM thermal histories, resulting phase fractions (including reversed austenite), and surface condition quantitatively interact to determine chloride-induced pitting across process parameter ranges and post-treatments. {{cite:S4#c1}}{{cite:S8#c1}}{{cite:S3#c1}}{{cite:S2#c1}}

