Household decisions about rooftop PV are strongly shaped by affordability and upfront cost barriers: several studies report that high initial investment and ongoing cost concerns deter adoption. Survey and modelling work finds that government subsidies reduce the initial risk and act as a primary motivator for household investment in PV, while low prices and low maintenance costs reduce long‑term return apprehension. {{cite:S3#c1}}  

Empirical analyses in lower‑income contexts emphasise affordability and payment flexibility as decisive for uptake. A nationally representative study finds that affordability determines the type of solar product adopted and that flexible payment mechanisms increase uptake among income‑constrained households, particularly in rural areas; the same study reports that grid access is negatively associated with uptake and that adoption patterns are heterogeneous by income, housing type and education. {{cite:S7#c1}} Survey‑based research using structural equation modelling also reports that perceived cost has a negative effect on willingness to adopt and that perceived financial risk reduces uptake. {{cite:S6#c1}}  

Awareness, technical knowledge and perceived risks are recurring non‑financial inhibitors across studies: techniques that combine SEM and ANN identify technical barriers and knowledge/awareness factors as significant predictors of investment decisions, alongside financial motivators. {{cite:S3#c1}} Field surveys of pilot projects likewise solicited consumer experiences and concerns about rooftop PV deployment, indicating that user experience and space availability are practical constraints encountered by consumers. {{cite:S8#c1}}  

Institutional and policy‑level barriers appear in the literature as both direct obstacles and sources of mitigation. Reviews and policy analyses describe national targets and policy instruments for rooftop PV, and they characterise a range of implementation challenges and broader constraints that hamper renewable energy development in the country. {{cite:S1#c1}} {{cite:S2#c1}} Policy reviews of the broader renewable sector summarise obstacles and recommend actions for policymakers and project developers. {{cite:S5#c1}} At the same time, critical work on equity highlights uneven demographic and spatial impacts of household solar expansion and calls attention to distributional risks that policy must address. {{cite:S4#c1}}  

Where studies converge, they show that both financial (subsidies, price, payment flexibility) and non‑financial (knowledge, technical barriers, institutional arrangements) factors jointly influence household rooftop PV uptake, and where they diverge it is mainly across contexts and product types—e.g., household systems in Kerala versus national samples or international settings. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S7#c1}} {{cite:S6#c1}}  

The literature leaves unresolved how quantified upfront cost and subsidy interventions translate into adoption among rural Indian households, and which specific institutional or payment innovations most effectively overcome both financial and informational barriers in those settings; the closest empirical contributions are household studies in Kerala, a national modelling/survey analysis for Uganda, and a pilot survey in Puducherry. {{cite:S3#c1}} {{cite:S7#c1}} {{cite:S8#c1}}  
[[NEEDS SOURCE: quantified upfront cost and subsidy impact in rural India]]

